00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:04.230
a real impact story from your
data science work for the

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.660
university.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.510
- Yeah, that's a
great question. And so Patrick,

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.700
I'm guessing you have
experience in academia or in

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.805
higher ed. You seem to
know the space pretty well.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:19.650
Doing the data work
is the easy part.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:25.880
Getting people to actually use the
data and inform policies is much trickier.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.690
And I also always like to point
to the fact that it really does

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:34.010
take a village. We meet all
kinds of people to be invested

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.010
and interested in

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:37.970
a project.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.770
But there is one that we've
been pointing to lately,

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.670
which was just
released publicly to--

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:45.500
quietly because it
happened during finals.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.890
But one of the things that
we've thought a lot about is

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.460
ways to make it so that
students have as easy of an

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:56.870
experience as possible
because we really, really want

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:00.950
students to be successful, not
just because that's our core

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.470
mission, but we're
measured on that.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:06.110
All universities are measured
on that in a lot of ways.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.940
And students want to go to
a place where they're going to be

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.030
successful. So we're
constantly looking for

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:12.080
what I would consider--

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:15.080
and I know this is
gross terminology for people in

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.590
higher ed-- but

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:21.100
building a college degree
really is a supply-chain

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.070
problem. You need to have

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:25.840
all of these constituent parts.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.280
And they need to land
at the right time.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.840
And there's tons
of dependencies.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.580
And those dependencies need to
be built out years in advance.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.550
So if we don't have
enough chemistry labs,

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.600
that student doesn't
get to take chemistry.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.160
And if not enough students
take chemistry, they don't have the

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:41.770
required courses. And
they can't graduate.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.940
So we're constantly
looking for these things.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.690
And one of the things
that we have found

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:53.740
is that students found the
prices of their textbooks to

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.990
be something of a barrier.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:58.090
And one of the things
that we actually found--

00:10:58.090 --> 00:10:59.770
we're not only trying
to graduate students,

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:04.570
but we're trying to graduate
everybody as equitably as possible. If

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:08.020
the experience is one way for
some students in one way for

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:12.650
another group of students,
that is not considered success.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:13.660
And so what we were finding,

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:15.400
and what we had heard
and what we had learned,

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.920
is that students were
potentially choosing which

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.680
courses to take based on
the cost of the textbooks.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:24.400
And there's also a little bit
of this mode of understanding, now,

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:29.350
that the students of today
are used to subscription services.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:30.700
Nobody ever thinks, oh,

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:33.970
how many songs did I
listen to on Spotify?

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:36.400
Am I getting my money's
Worth there just used to--

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:40.360
or Netflix or these models,
where you just pay one price

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:41.480
and you get everything.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.350
And it doesn't matter how much you
use it or how little you use it.

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:45.940
So Patrick, to answer
your question--

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:47.890
this is the long way
around. But to answer your question,

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:50.020
we just recently deployed--

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.890
and you can read
about it publicly--

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:55.450
but one of the first handful,
maybe dozen or 15 schools,

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.490
that have gone in on this

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:00.430
bookstore-equity access
model, which is basically,

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:03.040
students pay one price.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.440
And that gives them the
financial stability,

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:06.950
the predictability
in their budgets,

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:08.560
their ability to
budget on books,

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.890
but also the ability to be
really flexible in what they take.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.890
And

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:15.480
by paying that one price,

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.540
they get what we call
day-one digital access.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:20.760
And there's also evidence
that students who--

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.070
think about your college days. You
go and you take a class.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.510
The professor gives the
old, look to your left,

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:26.970
look to your right, one
of you is going to fail.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.080
And you're like,
I'm out of here.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:30.720
And so then what you have
to do is, you have to go.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:31.920
You have to go back
to the bookstore.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.350
You have to return that
book. And so we have found--

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.480
and there's evidence
that having digital,

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.670
immediate access to your books

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.590
is actually a student
success lever.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:42.210
And so for a
variety of reasons,

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:44.680
we wanted to implement
this program.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.280
And one of the things that
we were asked to do is--

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.580
what price should
the bundle be?

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:51.720
And

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:53.350
if any of you have
worked in pricing,

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:56.850
it's a really complicated problem.
If the price is too high,

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.340
nobody participates. And
the whole thing collapses on

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.540
itself. And if it's too low,

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:03.370
you don't cover your costs.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.780
And so we had a whole team
of people who were thinking

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:08.190
through that problem.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.190
And

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:12.430
Rachael was very
clear. She's like,

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:15.250
you do not need to
shill for Posit.

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:18.460
But we are happy to say that
we used our super-sweet Posit

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:22.690
Workbench set up to simulate
a whole bunch of different

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.100
options. The Data
Science team did that.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:27.880
And we were able to

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.820
help them provide--

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.730
to guide them on what that
price was. And it got deployed

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:35.500
out. And so next year,
from what I understand,

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:40.360
all undergraduates at CU Boulder
will pay $279 a semester,

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:44.230
and get all of their books on day one.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.190
And if they drop a class,

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:46.930
then those books go away
and the new ones come in.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.880
And they just have
access to their books.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:50.420
Patrick,

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.370
I think it would be valuable to
follow up with me a year from

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:56.810
now and see how well we
did because this is-- it's

00:13:56.810 --> 00:14:00.970
a known unknown. We're going to
have to see where it landed.

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:02.680
- That is a great
example. Thank you very much. And

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:04.450
really, not what
I was expecting,

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:06.400
in terms of the
example you choose.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:10.510
But it just demonstrates how broad
the impact could be in an

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.270
area that's so complex
and diverse, like you described.

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:14.920
So thank you.
That is wonderful.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:18.190
- Yep. You've you got it.
Sorry, I need to read the chat.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:22.720
"--is less than I was paying
20 years ago." So that's-- yeah. So--

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:24.130
we'll

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:25.780
see how we do.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:30.430
- Yeah, that's amazing. There's a
lot of love for that example,

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:32.620
in the chat here, too.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:36.460
I see-- Abigail, you had
asked a question, in the chat,

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.160
a little bit earlier. Do
you want to jump in, here?

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:40.420
ABIGAIL: Thanks, Rachael. Yeah,

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:42.820
this is something multiple
organizations I've been part of

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:46.010
have been trying to figure
out, what to use for databases.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:50.920
So I see you guys are doing a
lot of public dashboards on top of public.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:52.840
How did you think that
through, an organization--

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:54.840
what DataBiz tools to use?

00:14:56.150 --> 00:15:00.280
- So that is a great question.
I'm sorry, your name is

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:02.560
Abigail? I didn't see
where it got highlighted.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:02.800
ABIGAIL: Yes.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.400
- Awesome. So thanks
for that great question.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.380
DataBiz is

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:12.760
eternally complicated
because the

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.590
technology is always moving.

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:16.450
And

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:20.020
then I think it's also fair
to point out that higher ed tends

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.060
to be a fairly
conservative industry.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:25.520
And it tends to buy
things in big ways.

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:28.260
So hey, we want to spin up

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.370
a Posit server,

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.920
and just test it
out on 10 users,

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:35.980
is usually a little bit
different than, what

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:39.610
is the best way that we
can use our tremendous buying power to

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:42.170
have everybody
on the same tool?

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:43.800
And so

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:47.310
it's always striking
a balance. But

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.025
a lot of universities have
gone in pretty deep on--

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:56.380
I don't know if I'm allowed
to say it-- but on Tableau.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:16:01.450
If a third of you hang up right
now, I will not blame you.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:02.840
RACHAEL DEMSPEY: You can
say whatever you want.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:05.890
BRAD WEINER: No, I
know. But I'm just--

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:07.750
so anyway,

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:10.750
Tableau is an industry
standard across what's called

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:12.970
institutional research. So

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.070
universities have all these
compliance regulations.

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:17.380
They need to report out
a bunch of stuff. And

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.170
so Tableau is just
pretty commonly used,

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.100
and has been for about
the last 10 years.

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:26.460
So we do use--

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:31.470
no comment, Bill, but
you're potentially correct.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.920
But one of the
things that we're constantly thinking

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.350
through are ways to

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.150
leverage more
modern technology.

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:42.240
And then one of
the things that's

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.690
a really interesting problem
space is that Tableau

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:50.340
doesn't necessarily provide semantic

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:53.580
HTML underneath, as
part of its API calls.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.070
And what that means is
that it's actually a little bit

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:59.860
difficult to access with
screen readers. And of course,

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:02.050
we're a public university. We put
out a bunch of public data.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:05.069
And one of the things we want
to make sure we do is have as

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:07.560
accessible of

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:12.130
visualizations and accessible access
to our information as possible.

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.130
And so

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:18.700
Tableau provides a ton of
value for a lot of reasons.

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:21.790
And people really like being
able to explore the data.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:25.270
But we are also looking at

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.460
other ways to potentially

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.860
visualize information
and put out data.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:35.290
There's also just people who want
to use open-source tools. And so

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:38.250
there's always the
conversation of

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:39.420
how

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:43.800
big can we scale things
like Flask or Streamlit or Shiny?

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:47.310
And I know that those
are amazing options. And I love

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:54.190
them. But we haven't gotten to
a point of institution-wide buy in.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:55.590
So we pick,

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.970
Abigail, in the way that
we pick a lot of things,

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.690
which is through a
process called satisficing, which is

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:03.540
like, well, the university
up the road is doing it.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.390
It seems good enough.
It'll be fine.

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:07.680
We'll go with that.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:11.580
And then we're mostly
happy with what we get

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:13.580
until something
better comes along.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:20.450
And all of the open-source
questions, I totally agree with.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:22.890
And we just haven't
gotten there yet.

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:24.800
So those of you who work
in your school start-up,

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.570
with 50 people, and
you're like, oh, well,

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:28.610
we were in Streamlit for three
weeks and it didn't work for

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:32.180
this, now we're going to pivot
over to Shiny or whatever,

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:36.170
it's just a lot harder for us
to move the gravity of such a

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.660
big organization.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:42.470
And I will say Tableau has a
lot of advantages. It has a lot

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:47.360
of functionality, although it does
not have a spell check. So

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:50.310
what does that mean?

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:51.470
- I

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.890
see Lisa had a follow up
to your story, that you shared,

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:59.360
about the book pricing.
Lisa, do you want to jump in?

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:01.600
- Sorry. I'm
lunching right now,

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:05.820
which is rather early for
Minnesota. But-- so it is.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.130
Yeah, I was just curious
if you shared out--

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:12.060
and maybe it's a little
too early-- any of the

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.950
findings about

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:17.070
the books because I
know, having been in academia,

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:20.820
that at least [? McAlister
?] was thinking about that

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:22.920
problem, as well.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:25.420
- Yeah, we're still
pretty new to it.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.210
And

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:28.410
higher ed,

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.770
seemingly, never has a
mythical first mover.

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.960
But there are a couple of
schools that have gone in on

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:37.390
this, including the University
of California at Davis.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:42.930
And so they're the ones who
have the most information about it.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:45.510
I would be happy to share and

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:48.220
talk with the people
who actually built this out.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:51.060
But right now, we are
launching it for this fall.

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.850
And so it'll be a little
while before we have any real outcome

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.490
data. But of course, the
really exciting part will be,

00:19:56.490 --> 00:20:00.750
can we measure whether
or not that particular intervention

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:03.780
had an impact on
student success?

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:05.880
And so probably a year
or two years from now,

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.390
we'll have some real data on

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:09.770
what it all meant. Right now,

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.500
we were just trying to get the
thing out the door and put a

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:16.595
price on it. But all the
cool stuff is yet to happen.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.160
Also, hi, Lisa.

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:22.840
I know Lisa from--

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:26.540
tangentially, through the Twin
Cities data science community.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:28.390
So it's good to see

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:30.390
my Minnesota peeps in here.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:34.320
LISA: Likewise.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:36.120
- I see--

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:37.560
oh, sorry. I see, Allen,

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:40.900
you had asked a question a
little bit earlier in the chat.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:43.180
Do you want to
jump in here next?

00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:44.920
- Yeah, sure. Hi, Brad.
And hey, everybody.

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:48.430
It's super fun today.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:52.120
Brad, I'm really curious
where your role lives, within

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:55.660
the institution. Are you
strongly connected to IT? Are

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.160
you in administration?

00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:59.380
And I've got to think
that where you live,

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:02.620
organizationally, has got to impact
on how work comes to

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:05.890
you, how you get to decide
what to do, how you're prioritized,

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:09.430
all those kinds of things. If you
can expand on that, that'd be great.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:13.450
- Yeah, that's a great
question, Allen. And I work in

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.450
the administrative side.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.220
But I have a really good--

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:22.260
I have a colleague who's
a chief data officer.

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:25.680
And he will basically say that if
you run or work with a data

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:29.160
organization-- and I would
probably apply this to

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:31.950
any of your
organizations, although I

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:34.230
know my industry best--

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:37.860
but if you are a centralized
data team, and you're not

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:41.730
serving everybody,
you're failing.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:44.430
And maybe that's a little bit
harsh because it might not be

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:48.600
set up that way everywhere.
But for us, we want to support

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:51.540
academic units.
We want to support

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:52.620
academic leadership.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:55.980
We want to support colleges
and schools and programs just as

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:58.770
much as we want to support
the auxiliary units, parking,

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:02.100
and the bookstore, and finance,
and budget, and enrollment,

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:03.700
and all of those things.

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:04.800
So

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:09.420
our role, in the most
big-picture way, is to support

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:12.750
students, faculty, and staff,

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:16.230
and to engage in those three
missions as much as we possibly

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:21.730
can. And parsing out where the
boundaries are is really, really tough.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.830
So people on my
team, for instance,

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:25.650
we have a whole team
that does surveys.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:28.650
And those surveys
help understand

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.620
how students are

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:34.050
engaging on campus, what
their sense of belonging is,

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:36.160
what type of activities
they're participating in.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:37.320
But one of the things we do is,

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:40.590
we run what are called the
faculty course questionnaires

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:41.460
for all of campus.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:44.970
So every time you get to the
end of semester and you get a--

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.610
this professor was
great or this professor

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:51.360
wasn't so great, all of
those data are publicly available,

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:54.840
and come through this data group.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.360
And that provides us
opportunities to really talk

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:00.420
about student success through
the lens of teaching and

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:03.600
learning, and
through the lens of

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:07.270
how students are engaging in
the classroom. And then of course,

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:09.690
there's a whole discipline
of what's known as learning

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.820
analytics. So if
you Google that,

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:14.700
there's all kinds of
papers that are out there.

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:17.820
But the degree
to which students

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:19.980
in different classes

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:25.070
perform on different types of
exercises or different types of--

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.160
in

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:28.650
different types of
learning environments--

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:32.310
also matters. So

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:33.760
a bunch of years ago,

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:36.930
I had the opportunity to
work with a multi-institutional team

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:39.150
from around the Big Ten.

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:43.530
And we were trying to
understand whether or not

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:48.060
there was any difference in
academic performance based on a

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:53.520
student self-reported gender identity
in introductory STEM classes.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.470
And it was really
fascinating because one,

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:58.080
it was an amazing data
engineering problem. You're like, well,

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:00.300
we have a five-hour chemistry
class and they have a

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.620
three-hour chemistry
class. Are those the same?

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:06.120
So like harmonizing data across
a bunch of universities was

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.940
insanely complicated. But

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:12.240
it delved really deeply into

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:15.660
the unit of analysis of
the individual course.

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:17.430
And then, of course,
you can go even lower.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.510
What about the section?
What about the instructor?

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:20.850
And we

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:23.770
are here to support efforts
like that, as well--

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:29.330
which, by the way, is why
this job is so epically fun, because

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:31.160
for three weeks, you're
talking about bookstore prices.

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:34.310
And then you're trying
to figure out why

00:24:34.310 --> 00:24:36.320
courses that have just a
midterm and a final are

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:39.670
different from courses that have a
variety of assessments along the way.

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:41.790
So--

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:45.330
I don't know. That was a
long answer. But the gist is,

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:47.550
we live where we live,
organizationally.

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:50.610
But we have touch points
across the university.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:54.540
And if we didn't,
we wouldn't have

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.750
as good of an ability to answer
the really hard questions,

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:01.530
where those two things land
in exactly the same spot.

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:05.280
A student might not be
successful because they might

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:07.020
have basic-needs problems.

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:09.420
They might need
additional financial aid.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:12.150
They might not have
a sense of belonging.

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:14.220
They might not be
engaged in a community.

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:17.130
They might also just really
not be that good at math,

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:21.510
and need help with that. And
all of that is part of the

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:23.820
interconnected

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:25.820
"supply chain."

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:27.710
- That's great. Thank you.

00:25:27.710 --> 00:25:30.950
It sounds like such a
really fascinating place to

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:36.110
work with, so much variety
of problems and teams and people--

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:38.630
juggling a ton. But it
sounds really, really neat. Thanks.

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:42.380
- Yeah, of course.
It's completely wild.

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:45.290
But it is fun. It's an
incredible amount of fun,

00:25:45.290 --> 00:25:46.676
definitely.

00:25:46.676 --> 00:25:47.040
RACHAEL DEMSPEY: Yeah,

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:50.670
it's really interesting to hear
about the different variety of

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:53.310
problems you get to work on.

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:56.850
I think, when we were
talking a bit earlier, Brad,

00:25:56.850 --> 00:25:59.260
you mentioned that

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.090
there aren't that
many schools, today,

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:03.760
that have a chief data officer.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:09.310
And I was wondering, for schools
who are maybe in that spot

00:26:09.310 --> 00:26:13.420
of starting out, or starting
this role of chief data

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:15.880
officer, if you have
any tips to share them.

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:20.170
- Yeah,

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:22.360
I get this question

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:24.400
pretty often.

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:26.170
And

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:27.820
so I'm going to get my
numbers a little bit wrong.

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:31.930
But there's roughly 4,000
or 5,000 institutions in the

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:35.290
United States that
receive Title IV funding.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.210
Maybe it's actually probably
a little bit higher.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:37.960
But that's everything,

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:43.430
from the California Barber
College to Stanford.

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:44.500
And that's basically--

00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:47.680
Title IV funding means
that they're eligible for Federal

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:50.770
Student Loans or--

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:51.820
well, that's probably it.

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:54.280
They're probably in the
Higher Education Act,

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:56.440
which Congress
never reauthorizes.

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:59.140
So we're always under
20-year-old rules.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:01.090
But there's thousands and
thousands and thousands of

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:04.630
universities. And I'll just get
on my soapbox for four seconds,

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:09.310
to say, remember that number
the next time you read, in

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:10.120
The New
York Times, or

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:10.810
The Wall
Street Journal,

00:27:10.810 --> 00:27:11.470
or The Washington

00:27:11.470 --> 00:27:14.470
Post, that it's so
hard to get into college.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:15.620
It's not.

00:27:15.620 --> 00:27:19.870
We have so many choices.
And most schools admit most

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:22.720
students. So I always
like to just tell people,

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:26.350
the national media does not
paint a very good picture of

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:29.940
the higher-ed landscape at all.

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:32.670
Off the soapbox, of those,

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:35.780
from what I understand,
there's probably

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:41.300
50 or so chief data officers
or fewer people with that title.

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:43.640
There is a very traditional
function, in higher ed,

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:47.120
called institutional
research, which

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:48.890
reports out all the numbers
to the federal government.

00:27:48.890 --> 00:27:52.500
And they typically
do this kind of work.

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:53.810
So

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:56.210
I'm not trying to
pick on you, Lisa. But

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.310
at Macalester, in Saint Paul,

00:27:58.310 --> 00:28:00.530
they actually have a
really amazing institutional research

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:03.020
office that's run by
really cool people.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:05.420
But if you've worked in higher
ed, you've, at some point,

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:07.520
bumped into your IR group.

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:10.130
If you want to know
anything about a university,

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:11.970
these data are
publicly available.

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:14.180
And so there's someone who
submits all of those data.

00:28:14.180 --> 00:28:16.280
And it's usually IR.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.910
But what's happening
more and more is,

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:22.060
institutions are realizing
that their data is this core,

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.320
institutional asset,

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:28.270
and that the more value that
can be gotten out of those data,

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:30.520
the better the institution can do
and the better that they can

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:31.880
support students.

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:35.200
There's also a growing sort
of sense that with the regulatory

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:37.390
framework and with compliance
frameworks and with the need

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:40.300
for data governance and with
the need for more standardized

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:42.490
policies around
data, that-- oh,

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.280
look we have an IPEDS
key holder. Look at that. Cool.

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.770
Nice to see you, George--

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:49.690
but

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:54.790
the conversation around, how
do we broadly manage our data,

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:57.850
is something that a chief
data officer should be responsible for.

00:28:57.850 --> 00:29:01.120
And I wouldn't say it's like
a thing. But I'd say that,

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:02.770
probably, once a month,

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:05.710
some university reaches out to
me on LinkedIn and is like,

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:09.140
should we have a chief
data officer? What does that mean?

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:11.470
What does the construct
look like in higher ed?

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:14.810
And just

00:29:14.810 --> 00:29:16.820
like it was a growing trend
in industry, probably,

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:22.500
over the last 10 years, it's
now catching up in higher ed.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:23.940
And yes, there's a
little bit of a chat.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:28.500
IPEDS is the Integrated
Postsecondary Education Data System.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:32.310
If you want to know
the number of enrolled

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:35.700
Asian students at the
University of Texas, Paso,

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:39.880
it's all in there. And
it's publicly available.

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:40.770
- Thank you.

00:29:40.770 --> 00:29:42.040
BRAD WEINER: Yep.

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:44.230
- I always do a double
check on acronyms.

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:45.905
BRAD WEINER: Yep.

00:29:45.905 --> 00:29:46.210
RACHAEL DEMSPEY: [? Deepsha, ?]

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:47.780
I see you had a
question in the chat.

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:50.350
Do you want to
jump in here next?

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:52.210
- Thanks, Rachael. If
I get a cough attack,

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:55.060
I might hand it back to
you, to complete the question, but--

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:56.680
- I'll be ready.

00:29:56.680 --> 00:29:58.570
- This has been
really fascinating,

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:01.060
to learn about the setting
of university and the kind of

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:02.980
problems that you have.

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:05.530
I'm a data scientist
at Microsoft. And usually,

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:06.490
in a business setting,

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:10.360
I know the challenge that
you spoke of is someone actually

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:14.530
taking the action on the
analysis you're doing, is a

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:15.920
really big challenge.

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:20.260
And so on here, a lot of
times, we don't pick up analysis that

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:23.410
could be interesting because
we don't have the identified

00:30:23.410 --> 00:30:25.780
stakeholders that would
actually take the action,

00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:28.960
or willing to take the action.
But it's also chicken and egg.

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.410
If they don't see the analysis,

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:33.830
they don't know what possible
action they could take.

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:36.400
So how do you overcome
that challenge?

00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:39.400
How do you pick out the
projects? How do you prioritize them,

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:42.850
considering you mentioned such
a vast variety of projects

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:44.850
you're able to take?

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:47.860
- Yeah, I would say--

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:53.030
carefully and imperfectly.

00:30:55.370 --> 00:30:57.500
And I think it's-- one
of the coolest things--

00:30:57.500 --> 00:30:59.630
and I have to give a hat tip
to Posit and to Rachael--

00:30:59.630 --> 00:31:02.540
one of the coolest things about
getting in a room like this is

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:06.260
realizing that we all
have the same challenges.

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:09.110
I always had this idea,
like, oh, you work at Amazon,

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:12.140
you work at Microsoft. They've
solved everything. Right?

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:13.850
And I've worked in higher ed.

00:31:13.850 --> 00:31:17.450
This place-- it's
been here since 1876.

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:20.180
It's not going anywhere
soon. But it's certainly

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:23.030
decentralized and
compartmentalized and has those

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:25.190
same type of
organizational challenges.

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:26.510
And I'm not bagging on CU.

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:28.040
I'm bagging on--

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:29.210
I'm not even bagging on.

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:34.680
I'm describing the reality of
being in a university environment.

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:36.680
And I would say that

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:47.200
mostly, we take on projects in
what will ostensibly have the largest

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:52.490
potential impact for students or
for what we're trying to do.

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:56.650
There's also a little bit of
a conversation that I think I've

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:58.900
thought deeply about,
[? Deepsha, ?] which is,

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:01.750
what is within your
span of control?

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:05.320
And sometimes it's not-- and

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:08.560
this might come across as

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:12.730
the easy road out. But
it's like, what are things that

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:17.050
we could directly
impact or influence?

00:32:17.050 --> 00:32:19.750
And then there are
also times where

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:21.400
the decision is made

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.620
in ways that I refer to as,

00:32:23.620 --> 00:32:27.070
you're bringing water to
the desert, where occasionally,

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:31.570
there are people that have
nothing. They are just totally

00:32:31.570 --> 00:32:34.090
out in the open. And
so on that dimension,

00:32:34.090 --> 00:32:37.690
of what's easy for us versus
what's valuable to them,

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:41.270
is such in the sweet spot
because this simplest,

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:42.910
most basic thing--

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:45.220
it's like someone's just
been asking forever,

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:48.910
all I need is this one thing and
to be able to get at it, routinely.

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:50.480
And you're like,

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:53.380
hold my beer. Let's focus

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:57.640
the sprint. Let's focus the
cycles on that one thing.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:33:00.670
And those tend to be the
situations where people come

00:33:00.670 --> 00:33:04.150
back and they're like,
thank you so, so much.

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:06.790
We just needed
this one dashboard.

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:08.790
So I would say, if

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:13.470
there's a thing that's easy for
you and high value to someone

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:15.420
else, that's worthwhile.

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:17.420
If there's something
where you really feel like

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:23.300
it's totally within your
galaxy to be like, hey,

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:24.910
we had this finding,

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:27.730
that finding suggests we
shouldn't do this again,

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:31.430
you're the person who's
directly responsible for that,

00:33:31.430 --> 00:33:35.810
don't do that again, and
that's right within your

00:33:35.810 --> 00:33:39.950
sort of frame of reference
or within your span of control--

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:43.130
And then, of course,
as someone who

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:47.000
just-- in case you can't
tell, I'm a wacky guy.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:51.590
I really value fun and joy and

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:55.760
being a little bit weird,
which I think is OK, and that

00:33:55.760 --> 00:33:59.360
one tiny corner of your
whole portfolio should just be that

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:02.360
random thing that seems like it's
going to be a ton of fun

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:04.670
to work on because those are
the kinds of things that keep

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:06.710
people engaged.

00:34:06.710 --> 00:34:10.070
And a lot of times, it
isn't fun in some whimsical way.

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:13.219
But it's just new, like
this bookstore thing.

00:34:13.219 --> 00:34:16.080
That was hard work. It
was a complicated problem.

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:17.300
But it was probably

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:19.170
five weeks of work,

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:21.500
it was time boxed. And
it was that one thing.

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:24.080
And then we passed it
on. And we're like, oh,

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:24.710
that was cool.

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:27.590
We got to run tens of thousands
of simulations on student

00:34:27.590 --> 00:34:28.940
opt-out behavior.

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:31.130
Now back to our regularly
scheduled program.

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:34.909
And we can focus on
the core stuff again.

00:34:34.909 --> 00:34:36.570
So is that helpful?

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:39.020
- No, it definitely
is. And it's

00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:42.620
great to see how that
value-to-effort constant

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:46.429
measurement has to be done
in almost any setting.

00:34:46.429 --> 00:34:48.300
It's common and it's constant.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:50.210
So thank you so
much for sharing.

00:34:50.210 --> 00:34:50.299
-

00:34:50.299 --> 00:34:52.370
And you did ask another
question about how do you

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:54.710
convince people
to use the data.

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:58.530
And that is, I think--

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:05.280
it just takes time. And it
takes trust. And it takes effort. And

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:09.660
it's also a recognition that it
is not a linear path of, we're

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:11.010
getting better and
better and better,

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:13.140
and every single time
there's more trust.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:15.510
You go from step one to
step two, and that's great,

00:35:15.510 --> 00:35:17.130
and step two to step
three, and that's great.

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:18.480
And then people start
coming to you more.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:21.090
And then you totally botch something.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:23.940
And you're back to step
one because the thing went

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:25.860
sideways. And so

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:29.670
I like to think that
organizations are

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:33.450
more resilient than we think
they are and that mistakes are

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:37.470
less damaging than we think
they are, in most cases.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:39.680
And so

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:41.870
people shouldn't be too hard
on themselves. If you're like,

00:35:41.870 --> 00:35:44.540
we found this thing, and
then people go, oh, that's cool,

00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:48.290
right on, we're still going to
do this other thing, and you're like,

00:35:48.290 --> 00:35:51.350
but you might not want to
do that because the data say this--

00:35:53.430 --> 00:35:59.810
And I will say that higher
ed is especially prone to folklore and

00:35:59.810 --> 00:36:01.280
to

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:04.730
people believing that their
experience is outweighing data.

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:06.570
That's changing a lot.

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:08.450
But one of the most fun
parts about working in higher ed--

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:12.420
and this is one of the things that
I was talking to Rachael about-- is that

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:13.680
if you work in some industries,

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:18.210
finding the inefficiencies is really
hard because all of the

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:23.220
inefficiencies have already been found.
And in higher ed, they're

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:26.730
all over the place.
So it's pretty cool.

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:29.040
But to give you guys a
quick story about folklore,

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:32.130
has anyone ever heard the myth
that students choose which

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:35.010
college they're going to go to
based on the weather on the day

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:39.310
that they visit campus? Has
anyone ever been down that one?

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:40.630
- I have.

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:41.880
BRAD WEINER: Someone has?

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.060
[INAUDIBLE] has. I really wanted
to go to School X.

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:48.700
But it was rainy and terrible.
There's no way I'm going there.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:50.170
So

00:36:50.170 --> 00:36:52.240
it's maybe a little bit
of an admissions joke.

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:54.840
But there is some

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:56.760
mythology around that.

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.920
I happen to used to work at
the University of Minnesota.

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:02.310
Minnesota has famously
great weather.

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:07.390
And I tested that assumption. And
it turns out to be false.

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:11.950
And that included pulling
hour-by-hour data--

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:14.830
weather data-- for
every single day,

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:16.030
going back years
and years and years,

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:18.190
and matching that up with
when students visited.

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:20.230
But to make it even
more interesting--

00:37:20.230 --> 00:37:21.700
I know I'm talking to
a bunch of data people.

00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:23.530
So you're all going to be
like, how did you do that?

00:37:23.530 --> 00:37:26.020
I actually calculated,
not the weather on campus,

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:28.960
but I calculated the delta
of the weather on campus from

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:31.660
where they came because I'm
imagining someone from Southern

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:35.050
California being like, wow,
this is a shocking difference.

00:37:35.050 --> 00:37:37.480
And I modeled that

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:40.750
in every possible
way. It was full-on

00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:42.220
P-hacking? data torture.

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:46.550
And I couldn't find anything
to support that assumption.

00:37:46.550 --> 00:37:50.890
So the next time you
hear that one, college admissions people,

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:53.530
I tested it in truly one
of the worst climates,

00:37:53.530 --> 00:37:55.450
or the most extreme climates,

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:58.800
in America. And it's not true.

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:03.550
- That it's such an
interesting analysis. I absolutely love it.

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:04.890
BRAD WEINER: Thank you for
your great question, [? Deepsha. ?]

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:06.111
- Thank you.

00:38:06.111 --> 00:38:08.650
I see Michael had asked
a question in the chat.

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:11.890
And there's stars so I'm
going to read it. But it was,

00:38:11.890 --> 00:38:14.890
"Brad, do you have any
experiences or stories in using

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:18.130
data science techniques
to attempt to measure the

00:38:18.130 --> 00:38:22.040
effectiveness of CU's strategic
communication efforts,

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:24.520
particularly in terms
of things like"-- well,

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:27.370
I guess this past example
could tie to this-- but,

00:38:27.370 --> 00:38:29.410
"particularly in terms
of things like recruiting

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:32.200
effectiveness or public
sentiment to university

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:34.540
outreach efforts?"

00:38:34.540 --> 00:38:37.030
I'll copy the question over
into the chat again, too.

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:38.540
- Yeah, that's a
great question.

00:38:38.540 --> 00:38:42.610
We have a lot of
connections with what, at universities, is

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:44.920
called enrollment management.

00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:48.610
And they ask questions
like that all the time.

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:50.080
And

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:54.280
I don't want to go too much
into the details because

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:56.530
it's

00:38:56.530 --> 00:38:59.560
complicated to tease out.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:04.780
But that is a really common set
of questions, that are asked, which is,

00:39:04.780 --> 00:39:06.910
really, what is the
effectiveness of anything?

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:09.580
What is the effectiveness
of a virtual visit versus an

00:39:09.580 --> 00:39:12.700
on-campus visit? What
is the effectiveness of

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:16.884
an email versus a
paper mailing? And those

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:24.950
are the types of things
that are routinely asked, and that

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:28.880
if you work at an
organization that does sort of marketing

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:31.700
work or does communications work,
those are also, I think,

00:39:31.700 --> 00:39:35.330
some of the highest-value
conversations to have in

00:39:35.330 --> 00:39:38.390
data-science land
because so often,

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:43.310
what we do is very tough to
append a specific cost or a specific

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:45.720
ROI to.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:50.390
And so there are things like
that, where you're like, look, we

00:39:50.390 --> 00:39:52.880
sent out this mailer. Did
it work or did it not?

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:55.610
We sent out this

00:39:55.610 --> 00:39:57.590
email communication. Did
it work or did it not?

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:58.610
Or what was the effect of that?

00:39:58.610 --> 00:40:00.710
Or what was the effect of
that on different students?

00:40:00.710 --> 00:40:02.180
And

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:05.890
email is basically
free. So it's an

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:09.850
arcane and not very
important research question.

00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:13.070
But there are questions of

00:40:13.070 --> 00:40:19.280
this whole combination of
communications. What do those do?

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:24.350
And if they are found to
have limited impact, then

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:27.680
it's very-- it's easy,
or it tends to be easy,

00:40:27.680 --> 00:40:28.610
to make that argument, of,

00:40:28.610 --> 00:40:32.690
you really should pivot these
dollars to something else.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:33.920
And

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:36.410
enrollment management
is becoming increasingly

00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:38.660
scientific. It really is.

00:40:38.660 --> 00:40:40.100
There's a guy whose
name is Brock Tibert.

00:40:40.100 --> 00:40:42.080
He's going to be mad at
me, that I mentioned his name.

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:44.900
But he thinks of it as a
science, enrollment science,

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:46.970
because what you
have are, you have

00:40:46.970 --> 00:40:48.950
millions of high
school graduates.

00:40:48.950 --> 00:40:54.470
And you need to land a class
that is big enough to--

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:57.210
it needs to be big enough
to support your operations.

00:40:57.210 --> 00:40:58.640
It needs to be the right size.

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:01.100
But you also need to
have that across different majors and

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:02.920
different colleges and
different programs.

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:05.300
And you need balance across
all these different things.

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:06.860
So

00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:09.290
if you Google the concept
of enrollment management or

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:10.160
enrollment science,

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:15.770
you'll find a bazillion papers out
there on how this happens. And

00:41:15.770 --> 00:41:17.450
it starts with

00:41:17.450 --> 00:41:19.220
millions and millions and
millions and millions and

00:41:19.220 --> 00:41:21.220
millions of people, whose

00:41:23.050 --> 00:41:25.990
name gets entered into
a database, basically.

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:27.220
And

00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:30.850
this is also a
really fascinating conversation, now, because

00:41:30.850 --> 00:41:34.300
COVID had a dramatic effect
on this entire industry.

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:36.850
One of the big things was

00:41:36.850 --> 00:41:38.390
that many, many,

00:41:38.390 --> 00:41:43.300
many more schools are now no
longer requiring the SAT or the ACT.

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:44.500
And in so doing,

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:47.800
the SAT and ACT were the
primary source of a lot of

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:52.570
these names. And so even
getting the marketing apparatus

00:41:52.570 --> 00:41:56.860
going around student enrollment is
a different story than it

00:41:56.860 --> 00:42:00.340
was even three to
five years ago.

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:02.740
But the short answer is yes.

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:05.860
And you can find all kinds
of resources and information

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:08.230
online about that.

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:09.550
You can also look
me up on LinkedIn.

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:12.430
And I used to work at a startup
that did that very thing,

00:42:12.430 --> 00:42:14.590
which was email

00:42:14.590 --> 00:42:17.320
marketing campaigns to
prospective college students.

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:21.190
And we had a ton of research
and information on how to do that well.

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:27.430
- I'll share your LinkedIn in
the chat, here, in a second too.

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:32.110
- And I'm sorry if sometimes I
have to be vague. I probably-- I

00:42:32.110 --> 00:42:35.260
don't want to get myself in
hot water by sharing results that

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:37.890
are probably not shareable.

00:42:37.890 --> 00:42:39.890
- That's OK. We're
used to that.

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:44.250
- At least it's not
the sports people.

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:45.900
I'm a sports analytics person.

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:48.720
We did a thing and
it made us win.

00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:51.830
Sorry. I can't say anymore.

00:42:51.830 --> 00:42:53.870
- Yeah, that's a hard one.

00:42:53.870 --> 00:42:59.870
Scott, I see you
had asked a question earlier.

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:01.940
I think it was about Connect,
if you want to jump in.

00:43:04.150 --> 00:43:06.390
SCOTT: Yeah, sure.

00:43:06.390 --> 00:43:09.180
No, I appreciate this conversation.
So I'm also in the

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:12.990
higher-ed, data-analytics, and IR
world, over at Georgetown.

00:43:12.990 --> 00:43:16.770
I resonate with a lot
of comments about Tableau and thisspace

00:43:16.770 --> 00:43:20.700
and Power BI and everything
around it, Looker, you name it.

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:24.300
But curious-- I think I have

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:26.130
found a team of folks
who is really curious,

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:28.530
and sees an enormous amount
of potential in the open source

00:43:28.530 --> 00:43:31.830
space in the orbit of R
and Python and that sort of thing,

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:34.680
and was curious whether you
have a Connect server up and

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:36.990
going, whether you're working

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:38.730
with it in a
production fashion,

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:41.100
or whether you've
thought about it or

00:43:41.100 --> 00:43:42.510
were headed in that direction?

00:43:42.510 --> 00:43:46.740
And it isn't as common in
IR as I might have thought.

00:43:46.740 --> 00:43:47.640
So yeah,

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:51.190
just curious about whether
you've thought in that space.

00:43:51.190 --> 00:43:54.780
- We have. And I think
that it is certainly

00:43:56.880 --> 00:44:01.170
a direction we'd be interested
in pursuing, is going into

00:44:01.170 --> 00:44:04.170
open-source
visualization tools.

00:44:04.170 --> 00:44:07.950
We do have kind of an
inter-operable, multilingual

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:09.750
data science team.

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:11.730
And by the way, this is only
on the administrative side.

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:13.170
There are, of course,

00:44:13.170 --> 00:44:15.840
hundreds of academic units
out there, Scott, as you know.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:18.720
Who knows if the Physics
department has a massive server

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:22.110
on there, deploying everything.
Who knows? Very possible.

00:44:22.110 --> 00:44:22.860
Actually,

00:44:22.860 --> 00:44:26.010
to give you a sense
of how decentralized higher ed is,

00:44:26.010 --> 00:44:29.910
it was easier for me to
ask Rachael, which other people on

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:34.140
CU's campus were using Posit,
rather than finding out myself.

00:44:34.140 --> 00:44:37.940
So that's the world
that we're in.

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:40.410
RACHAEL DEMSPEY: Anybody wants to
know, they can always ask

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:43.570
me, for your own organizations.

00:44:43.570 --> 00:44:45.570
- So

00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:49.450
I honestly think that,

00:44:49.450 --> 00:44:51.730
as powerful and as great
as those tools are,

00:44:51.730 --> 00:44:55.810
we still haven't exactly figured
out how to make that happen.

00:44:55.810 --> 00:44:58.360
And some of that is

00:44:58.360 --> 00:45:02.020
tradition and sort of common
use of existing tools.

00:45:02.020 --> 00:45:05.890
And then some of that
is the deployment question, which is--

00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:07.450
one of the biggest
complaints you get,

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:09.220
when you're working in
a big organization, is,

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:13.000
please don't give me
something else to log into.

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:15.580
My bookmark bar is
already 900 things.

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:20.850
But Scott, I

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:24.380
would think that if

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:26.490
you have the appetite to build
it-- and then, of course,

00:45:26.490 --> 00:45:29.720
there's the cost
structure around it, where

00:45:29.720 --> 00:45:31.370
deploying something
in Posit isn't free.

00:45:31.370 --> 00:45:35.270
But it's definitely
cheaper than other options.

00:45:35.270 --> 00:45:38.150
But I would love to
connect and hear what

00:45:38.150 --> 00:45:39.800
your journey is like.

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:42.290
We do have a couple of
people who've build stuff in Shiny.

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:44.270
But then we go, well,

00:45:44.270 --> 00:45:46.940
some end user wants to
touch that and interact with it.

00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:51.050
And that end user is almost
certainly not going to have the

00:45:51.050 --> 00:45:57.520
skills to even like fire up
an IDE and run the framework.

00:45:57.520 --> 00:46:00.400
So we're probably in
early days on that.

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:06.210
But I think what's probably a
more likely path is that we would,

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:07.530
at least to start--

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:13.710
and this is all just me
brainstorming in front of 127 people

00:46:13.710 --> 00:46:18.330
on a high wire-- would be
that we would use some of the

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:21.780
semantic HTML frameworks that
come from open-source tools.

00:46:21.780 --> 00:46:25.658
So we would deploy using
something like Flex dashboard or something

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:33.280
like-- oh, my gosh, why is
my brain falling out of my ear--

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:36.640
Highchart, which is free

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:39.070
use, for academic institutions,

00:46:39.070 --> 00:46:42.760
to just deploy HTML web pages
and put those out there.

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:45.790
I think that would
be our gateway

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:48.355
into a full deployment
of something like Shiny.

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:54.480
- Yeah, thanks so much. And
I may ping you later because

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:56.370
again, we're at the front
end of this, and I think,

00:46:56.370 --> 00:46:58.410
on one hand, see a lot
of potential, but also,

00:46:58.410 --> 00:47:00.900
there's just a lot of
inertia elsewhere in the organization,

00:47:00.900 --> 00:47:03.360
as you well know and as
we've already talked about this morning.

00:47:03.360 --> 00:47:05.490
- Yeah, it's a hard problem,

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:09.120
to move people into a
new technical space. But Scott,

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:11.460
I could share some other paths
we're going down as well,

00:47:11.460 --> 00:47:12.550
which are pretty cool.

00:47:12.550 --> 00:47:13.740
- Yeah, great, thanks.

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:15.740
BRAD WEINER: Yep.

00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:20.420
- I see Peter, you had a
follow up question. I think it was,

00:47:20.420 --> 00:47:23.660
maybe, on the weather use
case. But I'm not sure.

00:47:23.660 --> 00:47:25.660
Do you want to jump in, here?

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:32.730
PETER: Oh, sure. I was just
wondering if your test,

00:47:32.730 --> 00:47:38.010
for that question, was looking
at all of the institutions that

00:47:38.010 --> 00:47:42.740
they were applying to, or if you
were only able to look at one.

00:47:42.740 --> 00:47:44.850
- Oh, man, if

00:47:44.850 --> 00:47:48.210
we had comparative data across
a bunch of institutions,

00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:49.570
that would be amazing.

00:47:49.570 --> 00:47:52.690
But it was just for
my single institution.

00:47:52.690 --> 00:47:53.550
And so yes,

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:56.400
I should be a much better
data scientist and put a bunch of

00:47:56.400 --> 00:47:58.290
asterisks at the end
of that description.

00:47:58.290 --> 00:48:02.400
That finding was limited
and localized and not generalizable

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:03.510
to any institution,

00:48:03.510 --> 00:48:06.360
beyond the single one that
I did the analysis on--

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:07.980
over the time period
that I did it on,

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:10.260
in the population
that I did it on.

00:48:10.260 --> 00:48:12.990
So fully

00:48:12.990 --> 00:48:14.990
great question.

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:17.130
Peter--

00:48:17.130 --> 00:48:20.370
because we have a bunch of

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:22.680
compliance rules-- there's a
federal law, called FERPA,

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:25.680
which is the Federal Education
Records Protection Act--

00:48:25.680 --> 00:48:30.090
there's a lot of stuff
that we can't share. And so

00:48:30.090 --> 00:48:32.790
getting a
multi-institution-level data

00:48:32.790 --> 00:48:37.800
set, at the unit of
analysis of student-level records, is

00:48:37.800 --> 00:48:41.750
really, really,
really complicated.

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:42.890
And

00:48:42.890 --> 00:48:45.410
institutions are just
very reluctant to share,

00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:46.760
not necessarily--

00:48:46.760 --> 00:48:50.030
We're very good at sharing--
so Scott just asked that question.

00:48:50.030 --> 00:48:51.800
And I have every confidence--
Patrick just asked the

00:48:51.800 --> 00:48:54.200
question-- that IR are people,

00:48:54.200 --> 00:48:57.300
or Institutional Analytics people-- you
all can call me up.

00:48:57.300 --> 00:48:59.780
And one of the best parts about
higher ed is we all have the

00:48:59.780 --> 00:49:03.080
same mission. We are
nominally in competition.

00:49:03.080 --> 00:49:06.980
But we're really trying to just
provide an economic ladder,

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:09.110
provide research, and make

00:49:09.110 --> 00:49:10.790
the world a little
bit better of a place.

00:49:10.790 --> 00:49:13.580
So we're widely collaborative. People
can call up and go, hey,

00:49:13.580 --> 00:49:14.150
how did you do this?

00:49:14.150 --> 00:49:18.300
And I will just straight
up tell people how that worked.

00:49:18.300 --> 00:49:19.820
But the data sharing,

00:49:19.820 --> 00:49:23.720
the ability to do
those multi-institutional analyses, is

00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:27.060
quite challenging
in a lot of cases.

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:28.790
But one of the things
we're actually working on, here at

00:49:28.790 --> 00:49:31.590
CU, is following after
the health care industry,

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:35.330
we are starting to develop
fully synthetic data sets that

00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:37.620
will allow us to do that.

00:49:37.620 --> 00:49:39.260
And I don't know many
institutions that are doing

00:49:39.260 --> 00:49:43.790
that. But I'm really excited
for a future where we could

00:49:43.790 --> 00:49:47.240
create synthetic data sets
that mirror this, essentially, the

00:49:47.240 --> 00:49:49.580
distributions that exist
in a real data set,

00:49:49.580 --> 00:49:53.840
but don't fall under the
data privacy and data protections

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:56.980
of federal law.

00:49:56.980 --> 00:49:58.900
But great question, Peter.

00:49:58.900 --> 00:50:00.940
I hope I answered
that sufficiently.

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:05.160
- Thank you.

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:07.750
AJ, I see you had just asked
a question in the chat.

00:50:07.750 --> 00:50:10.470
Do you want to
jump in here next?

00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:12.660
AJ: Yeah. Sorry if I
missed it, maybe, earlier,

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:13.830
if you mentioned it already.

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:17.910
But did your company start off
with having a data office? Or

00:50:17.910 --> 00:50:21.000
if not, what sort of
efforts it took to

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:22.860
create your role? Because

00:50:22.860 --> 00:50:26.100
today, a lot of big-- even
big companies sit on piles of data.

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:30.630
But data is something just
that's lumped under IT, normally.

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:33.510
Just wondering
your take on that.

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:35.250
- Yeah,

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:38.110
so I haven't been here,
at CU, for too, too long.

00:50:38.110 --> 00:50:41.640
So the history is, maybe,
a little bit murky. But

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:44.880
pretty much every university
has had an office,

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:47.880
that's typically
called institutional Research,

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:52.450
that has focused on these
analytic questions, and then, of course,

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:55.540
reporting to the various
agencies that require reporting.

00:50:55.540 --> 00:50:56.430
So here in Colorado,

00:50:56.430 --> 00:50:59.640
we have a bunch of requirements that
we report to the state of Colorado.

00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:03.690
We have a bunch of connections
with the city of Boulder,

00:51:03.690 --> 00:51:05.340
to make sure that
we're integrated there.

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:07.710
We have a bunch of
connections with

00:51:07.710 --> 00:51:10.200
the federal government
because of Title IV funding,

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:11.980
because of research funding.

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:14.010
So we have a lot of

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:16.770
data needs, to make
sure that we are being

00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:20.170
in compliance and that we're
being as effective as possible.

00:51:20.170 --> 00:51:26.490
So the tradition of having a data
office in higher ed goes way back,

00:51:26.490 --> 00:51:28.570
decades and decades
and decades.

00:51:28.570 --> 00:51:31.620
But as the appetite
for data has grown,

00:51:31.620 --> 00:51:35.220
and as the appetite for
people to make smarter decisions and

00:51:35.220 --> 00:51:38.430
more data-informed
decisions has grown,

00:51:38.430 --> 00:51:42.100
these data conversations have gotten
bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:51:42.100 --> 00:51:44.490
So do you have someone
who's there to deploy and analyze

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:47.700
surveys? Do you have
somebody who is there

00:51:47.700 --> 00:51:50.970
to do research on how
faculty are performing,

00:51:50.970 --> 00:51:53.310
to have to understand
how students are performing,

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:57.000
to really think through,
not just how many questions,

00:51:57.000 --> 00:52:00.840
but what is the relationship
between x and y questions?

00:52:00.840 --> 00:52:04.410
And then finally, the third
layer of that is just,

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:08.580
how do we make the best use
of our data in a secure and a

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:11.010
systematic way? How do we
democratize it? How do we scale

00:52:11.010 --> 00:52:12.520
it? How do we govern it.

00:52:12.520 --> 00:52:15.420
And so I would say that every

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:17.430
university, no matter
how big or small,

00:52:17.430 --> 00:52:20.820
the smallest community
college anywhere,

00:52:20.820 --> 00:52:22.820
probably has some--

00:52:24.610 --> 00:52:25.930
even if it's one person--

00:52:25.930 --> 00:52:29.800
some person who's
doing analytic work.

00:52:29.800 --> 00:52:35.640
But I think having
a centralized, systematic, networked

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:38.550
office, that's responsible
for thinking about data as an

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:41.280
asset, is probably
a growing trend.

00:52:43.290 --> 00:52:45.930
But I went to a Gartner
thing a year or two ago.

00:52:45.930 --> 00:52:48.180
And they had a little higher-ed
table. And it was like, oh,

00:52:48.180 --> 00:52:49.590
so you're the 6.

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:51.060
But I imagine that you
go a year from now,

00:52:51.060 --> 00:52:52.830
and there will be 12. And
I imagine you go a year from

00:52:52.830 --> 00:52:54.990
then, there will be 40. And

00:52:54.990 --> 00:52:58.410
higher ed is often
last to the party.

00:52:58.410 --> 00:53:00.330
But we

00:53:00.330 --> 00:53:03.930
track the trends that go on
in industry, quite often.

00:53:06.020 --> 00:53:07.220
- Thank you.

00:53:07.220 --> 00:53:09.470
As we-- oh, sorry--

00:53:09.470 --> 00:53:11.600
AJ: Oh, no, I was just
saying thank you. Thank you.

00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:12.900
BRAD WEINER: Of course.

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:14.330
- Thank you all for

00:53:14.330 --> 00:53:18.050
the great questions too. As we
get to the top of the hour,

00:53:18.050 --> 00:53:20.090
here, if there's
anything I missed,

00:53:20.090 --> 00:53:22.220
feel free to raise your
hand, here on Zoom,

00:53:22.220 --> 00:53:26.570
so I can see it as well,
and can call you in here.

00:53:26.570 --> 00:53:28.430
But Brad, one
question-- actually,

00:53:28.430 --> 00:53:31.140
one of my colleagues
had asked this earlier.

00:53:31.140 --> 00:53:32.960
But they mentioned they
saw something online,

00:53:32.960 --> 00:53:37.400
that you host a community of
practice event, and I was just

00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:43.510
curious to learn how that came
together and how you publicized it.

00:53:43.510 --> 00:53:46.420
- Yeah, that's a
great question. So

00:53:46.420 --> 00:53:49.960
there was an online
community of practice.

00:53:49.960 --> 00:53:53.590
There was a Zoom meeting
when I first joined CU.

00:53:53.590 --> 00:53:55.270
And

00:53:55.270 --> 00:53:57.340
what we found-- and I think
this gets back, a little bit,

00:53:57.340 --> 00:53:59.260
to [? Deepsha's ?]
question, which is,

00:53:59.260 --> 00:54:02.530
how do you get people to be in

00:54:02.530 --> 00:54:03.550
the experience?

00:54:03.550 --> 00:54:04.900
And

00:54:04.900 --> 00:54:07.300
the College of Engineering
has analysts. And

00:54:07.300 --> 00:54:09.040
all these places have analysts.

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:11.200
And they all have people
who are working on similar

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:14.830
problems. And so the
better we are able to

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:19.890
coordinate everybody and the
better we're able to work

00:54:19.890 --> 00:54:21.150
with everybody,
the better we do.

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:23.070
So we built out this
data community practice.

00:54:23.070 --> 00:54:25.200
And it was a Zoom thing.
And we invited some guests.

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:27.690
And I haven't looked through
the whole list, here.

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:31.650
But I may have hit some of you
up to be a guest at some point.

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:34.490
I know one person,
in particular.

00:54:34.490 --> 00:54:35.210
But anyway,

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:37.730
we'd have people come in
and talk about data governance or

00:54:37.730 --> 00:54:38.780
master data management.

00:54:38.780 --> 00:54:41.870
Or we'd have people come in
and talk about different things. And

00:54:41.870 --> 00:54:44.360
the idea surfaced of,

00:54:44.360 --> 00:54:46.850
we're all tired of staring at
each other on Zoom, a little bit.

00:54:46.850 --> 00:54:49.370
This is amazing because you're
all distributed all over the

00:54:49.370 --> 00:54:51.860
world. But we

00:54:51.860 --> 00:54:54.270
have a need to build
this community.

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:56.510
And so we decided to have
an event. And we actually,

00:54:56.510 --> 00:54:59.990
jokingly-- I name
things so stupidly.

00:54:59.990 --> 00:55:02.000
The biggest problem in computer
science is naming things.

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:04.070
It's why I'm never going
to be a computer scientist because

00:55:04.070 --> 00:55:06.140
I'm terrible at naming things.

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:08.720
But we straight-up
went with like IRL.

00:55:08.720 --> 00:55:12.050
We're going to have a data
community practice conference in real life.

00:55:12.050 --> 00:55:13.970
And let's all get into
the room together.

00:55:13.970 --> 00:55:16.340
And let's focus on a
couple of things. One,

00:55:16.340 --> 00:55:19.100
we wanted to do some
actual technical training and provide

00:55:19.100 --> 00:55:20.460
that value to our campus.

00:55:20.460 --> 00:55:24.080
So we had one training session
that was in R in the tidyverse.

00:55:24.080 --> 00:55:26.570
And we had another one
that was in Tableau.

00:55:26.570 --> 00:55:30.800
And it was just a chance
to take some real experts in that

00:55:30.800 --> 00:55:34.510
and lift a bunch of
boats all at once.

00:55:34.510 --> 00:55:36.740
And then we had some
broader conversations,

00:55:36.740 --> 00:55:39.040
including a keynote
speaker, who spoke

00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:43.180
about the need to think
about data in more equitable ways.

00:55:43.180 --> 00:55:47.890
We are constantly having
this conversation about-- these

00:55:47.890 --> 00:55:50.410
important conversations
around diversity and equity,

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:52.480
and around racism
and anti-racism.

00:55:52.480 --> 00:55:54.410
And

00:55:54.410 --> 00:55:57.020
believe it or not, if
you haven't spent time thinking

00:55:57.020 --> 00:55:59.990
about it, the way you collect
data, the way you capture data,

00:55:59.990 --> 00:56:03.030
the way you count
people matters.

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:05.960
And so we brought in someone
who is an expert in this--

00:56:05.960 --> 00:56:07.310
his name is Carson Byrd.

00:56:07.310 --> 00:56:08.810
We brought him in
from the University of Michigan--

00:56:08.810 --> 00:56:12.170
to really talk to everybody
and to have a thought-provoking

00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:15.860
conversation around the ways
that data may or may not

00:56:15.860 --> 00:56:20.690
reflect the communities that
we're trying to represent.

00:56:20.690 --> 00:56:22.670
And then we also gave
people the opportunity to do some

00:56:22.670 --> 00:56:25.790
lightning talks, which
were absolutely amazing, just--

00:56:25.790 --> 00:56:29.300
we invited people from around campus
to basically say, for 15 minutes,

00:56:29.300 --> 00:56:33.140
you have the opportunity to talk
about a problem that is vexing you.

00:56:33.140 --> 00:56:35.140
And

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:39.140
I cannot tell you
enough how cool it was--

00:56:39.140 --> 00:56:41.780
and I would recommend it to
any of your organizations--

00:56:41.780 --> 00:56:44.150
to not only stand
up a community of practice,

00:56:44.150 --> 00:56:46.370
and just get people
together and say, hey,

00:56:46.370 --> 00:56:48.470
what are you working on,
what problems do you have,

00:56:48.470 --> 00:56:52.070
but to occasionally do your best
to try and get everybody together,

00:56:52.070 --> 00:56:53.210
in

00:56:53.210 --> 00:56:58.010
a meaningful way, because people walked
out of that, in my view,

00:56:58.010 --> 00:56:59.750
not only technically better,

00:56:59.750 --> 00:57:03.110
but with the ability to sit
down and eat lunch with that

00:57:03.110 --> 00:57:07.140
person who sits across campus and
is working on the same thing.

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:11.730
So the Zoom thing came together
before me. But we were like,

00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:14.510
you know what? Let's get off
Zoom for a day and a half.

00:57:14.510 --> 00:57:16.520
And it was

00:57:16.520 --> 00:57:19.290
a ton of fun. Highly recommend.

00:57:19.290 --> 00:57:22.010
- I love that. I love
anything to do with community.

00:57:22.010 --> 00:57:25.280
That sounds awesome. OK. We
have a few minutes, here.

00:57:25.280 --> 00:57:28.860
So I might just rapid
the remaining questions to you.

00:57:28.860 --> 00:57:28.940
BRAD WEINER: All right,

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:31.350
I'll see if I can do
them in 20 seconds.

00:57:31.350 --> 00:57:32.390
Let's see how many
we can get through.

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:34.580
- [? Siring ?] asked,

00:57:34.580 --> 00:57:38.240
"have you done any analysis
of the chances of a student

00:57:38.240 --> 00:57:40.580
successfully finishing a course

00:57:40.580 --> 00:57:42.890
or failing out?"

00:57:42.890 --> 00:57:44.160
- Yeah, that's a really,

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:47.180
really common type of analysis
that happens, in higher ed,

00:57:47.180 --> 00:57:50.450
is trying to estimate
a student's likelihood. But

00:57:50.450 --> 00:57:54.410
again, we're not here to

00:57:54.410 --> 00:57:56.555
filter people out or
to make people feel--

00:57:58.880 --> 00:58:02.330
there isn't some channel for that.
What it allows us to do

00:58:02.330 --> 00:58:04.310
is, it allows us to
focus our resources,

00:58:04.310 --> 00:58:06.020
like any predictive model,

00:58:06.020 --> 00:58:09.770
on the students that might
need additional help.

00:58:09.770 --> 00:58:14.030
So if we can estimate that
a student might not be successful

00:58:14.030 --> 00:58:16.740
in a particular class, it
isn't, don't take that class.

00:58:16.740 --> 00:58:21.380
It's, what support can we give
you so that you are successful.

00:58:21.380 --> 00:58:23.800
- Thank you. And I think
you answered this one in the chat.

00:58:23.800 --> 00:58:27.760
But is there a likelihood
of colleges to move to

00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:29.950
